urses  not to hire nurses so I you know the the  percentage that this is ended on or  settled on will have to be resolved  around the negotiating table and I hope  that ministers will meet Pat Cullen uh  we'll talk about that but the precise  End Settlement has to be negotiated but  does 10 sound reasonable to you as a  broad principle would do you want to see  nurses enjoying a pay rise that's in  line with inflation when we were in  government we managed to raise nurses  pay we managed to reduce NHS waiting  lists and times and we left office with  the highest levels of public  satisfaction in the service ever  recorded and we never had a national  strike like no that's the approach that  we take but I'm not going to come out  today when I'm not a party to the  negotiations and say it should be this  percent or that percent I want nurses to  be fairly rewarded but the precise  figure has to be negotiated between  their Union and the NHS okay but I'm not  asking for precise figures but yeah  principles you're asking me to connect  to a percentage that's not wise for me  to do that's to be negotiated but this  isn't just a politician ducking the  issue this is how it should be  approached that should be negotiated  between the union and the employers  around the table okay so it's not doc  initially can I ask you if you think  that they should get more than the three  percent that's I mean that's quite a  broad range that could go to more than  three percent that's on the table now  well these things are usually settled in  this way the union will have asked for a  particular uh percentage or a particular  pay level at the beginning the employers  may have awarded something and usually  this is settled with a compromise where  maybe not everybody gets what they want  or what they said at the beginning of  the negotiation I think that's the  spirit in which it should be approached  but the precise figure it's settled on  has to be resolved on the table okay we  hovered to kiss armor yesterday laying  out he talked about Big Ideas he talked  about change and  people may take that on board but they  ultimately want to have  a clear answer on the key issues that  the country is facing now one of those  key issues is the strikes and they feel  they're not getting answers from labor  on this you don't want to put even a  broad Principle as far as nurses pay is  concerned well I think we have been  clear on the strikes we've said we don't  want these to happen we want the  government to roll its sleeves up and  get negotiating and to get the nurses  back to work get the trains running  again and in the longer term to improve  Public Services now if you take the NHS  for example we've produced a very clear  plan fully costed to train more doctors  and more nurses and answer the long-term  staffing issues that the NHS is facing  paid for by the abolition of the non-dom  tax break and that's the spirit in which  we'll approach these things we'll come  out with a policy we'll see how it's  going to be paid for and that's the  approach that we'll take at the next  election right now with this threat of  government legislation what we've got is  ministers making a pause rather than  striking a deal and it's striking a deal  that is what has to happen here well on  that anti-strike legislation it's kiss  number yesterday said that he would  repeal any new restrictions but  explain to people why it's such a bad  idea to have a minimum safety standard  as the government is is now calling it  when it comes to crucial Industries like  fire Rail and ambulance services why is  that such a bad idea  even the  Secretary of State for transport has  said that it would have no impact on the  current strikes and in the longer term  the department for transport's own  assessment of this legislation thought  it could lead to more disputes in the  future rather than fewer so what I think  what I see when I see the government  doing this is really an admission of  failure they are admitting that they've  failed to successfully resolve these  disputes so they are reaching for  legislation which they hope will form  some sort of dividing line it is  striking a pose rather than striking a  deal and striking a deal is what has to  happen to get these Public Services back  to work and beyond that  even when we don't have straight days  and we've got a lot of strike days right  now but even when we don't if you look  at Public Services right now can we see  it as a single public service that's  working better now than 13 years ago  when the conservatives came to power  they've gone backwards so the challenge  for ministers isn't just resolving the  current disputes it's what are they  going to do about public services in the  future  but there are already laws out there  that ban the police from striking and  they're already rules around nurses  going on strike they have to make sure  that they only walk out if it doesn't  risk the well-being of patients so do  you agree with those rules and is this  not just that along with there are lots  there are laws around uh striking  members have to vote for this in a  ballot notice periods have to be given  and so on these are not the kind of  Wildcat strikes that we saw in the 1970s  I wish they weren't happening I want to  see the waiting list coming down I want  to see the trains running and so on but  these these strikes have been voted for  by the members so I think the response  from ministers  shouldn't be a legislative one it should  be our negotiating one and we're not  seeing that at the moment and that's why  these are rumbling on now into six  months or more okay well Pat McFadden we  appreciate your time this morning thanks  so much for coming in thank you
 

